Nested rows: a row can hang under the one above it.

An application's desktop actions are details of that application, not peers of
it, and the launcher had no way to say so. The client was spelling the
relationship into the title with box-drawing characters, which put a glyph in
front of the text the filter matches against and still left every row the same
height and the same weight as the app above it.

So a row gains a nest depth. Depth 0 is what every row was; 1 and up draw
indented, at BM_NEST_SCALE of a full row's height, icon and font. Depth is a
property of the frame rather than of the item, which is what lets the client
nest its resting list and pass 0 for everything the moment a filter is typed,
when the parent a row hung under may not have matched at all.

Making rows different heights broke the paging, which was `index / lines *
lines` and only lines up when every row is the same height. lib/renderers/
rows.cpp cuts the list into pages by accumulated height instead. It is pure
arithmetic over an array of heights, so it is a C++ translation unit next to
pills.cpp for the same reason that one is, and nix/patches/beamenu/tests/
rows_fit_test.cpp exercises it under ASan and UBSan.

Paging rather than scrolling by the least amount that brings the highlight on
screen: with no memory of where the list sat last frame, "the least amount"
pins the highlight to whichever edge it entered from, so walking back up a
long list drags the run one row at a time and never shows what is above it.

The panel takes the height of the rows it will actually draw, but only when
the whole list is on screen. Hugging a scrolling list would resize the window
by up to a third of a row whenever paging changed which rows were visible.

Also adds the alternate-actions hint, drawn on the highlighted row when the
client marks it as having them. Only the highlighted row: the same glyph
repeated down a whole column is noise, and the panel has no footer to put it
in instead.

